00:06:08.797 --> 00:06:10.630
MARTIN GORNER: Let's
have a look at one TPU.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:11.860
What is inside?

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.500
I told you-- one board,
four chips, dual-core.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.570
What is in each of those cores?

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:20.950
So you have a fairly traditional
vector-processing unit.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:25.210
But the specific part that makes
this special for neural network

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:28.180
workloads is the
matrix multiply unit.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.660
It's a dedicated hardware
unit that performs 128

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.990
by 128 matrix multiplications.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:40.070
And it does so for speed in
reduced float16 precision.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:43.030
So this is a very traditional
trick in neural networks

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.130
to work in reduced precision.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.310
Neural network training is
fairly resistant to the loss

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.460
of precision.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.150
There are even cases where
the loss of precision,

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:57.100
by introducing noise,
actually helps the training.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:04.166
Sometimes you even
introduce noise on purpose,

00:07:04.166 --> 00:07:07.390
as a way of helping
the training.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:12.520
But the traditional-- I mean,
the standard float16 format,

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.760
as you can see here,
has a different number

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.620
of exponent bits compared to
float32, which means that it

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.630
doesn't have the same range.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.990
And so when you push your
model to reduce precision

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:29.950
using float16, you usually
get underflows and overflows.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.430
So all of these problems are
fixable if you know your code,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:34.780
but it's work.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.930
And in TPUs, we decided to
use a slightly different

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:41.680
reduced precision format,
which we called bfloat16.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.460
And you see, it's
actually float32 with just

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.260
the fractional bits cut off.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.320
It has exactly the same number
of exponent bits, exactly

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.170
the same range, which
means that it's usually

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:56.680
a drop-in replacement
for your float32s.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.260
And there is nothing to
be done on your model

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.070
when you use that.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:08.130
You actually don't have to do
the work of using bfloat16.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.260
This is something that
is done automatically.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.210
You send the data
in float32, and it

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:20.800
gets downgraded in
the MXU on the input.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.070
And then, the
multiplication between two

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.560
16-bit floating point
numbers becomes, again,

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:30.700
a float32, which is
what the result is.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.530
And now, how is this
unit built, the MXU?

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:39.409
So it uses quite a
rare architecture

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:45.030
in a microprocessors,
called a systolic array.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:49.440
Try to remember how to perform
a matrix multiplication.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.230
So in matrix multiply,
each point of the result

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.870
is a dot product of
one line of a matrix

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.310
and one column of
the second matrix.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:01.890
And a dot product is a series of
multiply-accumulate operations.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:05.310
Multiply, plus, multiply, plus,
multiply, plus, multiply, plus.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.840
So the only compute element
we have in this whole array

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.430
are multiply-accumulate
elements.

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:16.010
And I think you will have to
spend a little bit more time

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.720
offline with this
animation, but what

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.470
it does is that you load
one of the arrays into--

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.130
one of your matrices
into this array,

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.790
and you flow the numbers
from your second matrix

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:31.500
through this array.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.080
And if you-- I mean,
you'll have to believe me.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.370
As the data flows through it,
and as the multiply accumulates

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.850
are performed, and the
intermediate result gets passed

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.290
to the right, out of
this side will come out

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:50.040
all the dot products that
compose your resulting matrix.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.230
The nice part about
this architecture

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:55.830
is, well, first of all, all
the intermediate results

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.480
are just flowing on the wire--

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.700
so nothing to store
back to registers,

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.260
or to intermediate
memory, or anything else.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.480
Everything is
self-contained in here.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.480
And the second thing is that
the individual compute element

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.990
is a tiny, tiny 16-bit
by 16-bit multiply

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.460
and accumulate unit, which means
that we can cram lots of them

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.660
in one chip.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.260
And this 128-by-128
array contains

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.890
16,000 of those compute
units, of course,

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:28.920
with 32,000 in total.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.580
Density means power,
and power means cost.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.090
That's the advantage of TPUs.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.650
And, of course, you want
to know how fast this goes.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.590
So some of you might
remember my talk

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.350
from last year,
when I showed you

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.410
how to do airplane spotting.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:51.180
I'm using this neural network
as a benchmark these days.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.930
And so this neural network
trains in four and a half

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:58.200
hours, on a big powerful GPU.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.550
On a ML Engine AI
Platform, now, you

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.620
can easily get a
cluster of those.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.960
And with five powerful GPUs,
I can train this in one hour.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.760
And I've chosen this example
because this is exactly

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.320
the time it takes to train
it on one Cloud TPU v2.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.710
So just as a rule of thumb,
one cloud TPU, four chips--

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:23.040
roughly equivalent to
five powerful GPUs.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.490
In terms of speed,
in terms of cost,

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.880
it's almost three
times less expensive

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:32.550
because we have a
density advantage

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:35.720
in the microarchitecture.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.210
So now we have
powerful hardware.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:39.330
We also need a model--

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:40.510
an object detection model.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:46.590
We have a whole
library of models,

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:52.360
TPU optimized models, in
this GitHub/TensorFlow/TPU

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.960
repository.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.950
Nik, which one did you choose?

00:11:56.950 --> 00:12:01.180
NIKITA SPIRIN: We picked
RetinaNet, obviously since it's

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.530
an object-detection model.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:05.380
And we also picked
RetinaNet not only

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:07.990
because it is the
model available

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.320
in the official TensorFlow
report for object detection,

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.420
but because it is
considered to be

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:16.420
one of the best models for
object detection in general.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.692
And let's talk about it.

00:12:18.692 --> 00:12:20.900
MARTIN GORNER: So maybe you
can tell us how it works.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.277
Why is it the best?

00:12:22.277 --> 00:12:23.110
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:27.370
First, before we go deep
into the RetinaNet details,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:29.920
I will introduce the
overall pipeline that

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.260
is used for object
detection, and then we

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.540
will cover a special,
new idea that

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:37.480
was introduced in the
official RetinaNet paper.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.910
So overall, object
detection works

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:42.250
as a pipeline where
we first generate

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:43.510
a lot of bounding boxes.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:44.920
And you see them on the slide.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.560
Those are also called proposals.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.490
And then, we do prediction of
the class labels and adjustment

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:55.600
of the positions of the bounding
boxes using the neural net.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:59.530
And there are one-stage neural
networks and two-stage neural

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:04.220
networks, in the sense that,
in one case, for example,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:08.140
one-shot neural networks,
such as Overfit, SSD, YOLO--

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.870
they combine
candidate generation,

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:13.930
bounding box generation, and
detection stages together.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:19.150
They process images only once,
and that's why they are fast.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.120
At the same time, they
are not as accurate.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:25.240
And there are
two-stage detectors,

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.630
which separate those
two stages into two.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.940
They first generate
candidate proposals,

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:33.640
but then they do sampling
and post-processing

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:34.900
of the bounding boxes.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.360
And then, they already
predict the class labels

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.040
and adjust the coordinates.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:43.210
So RetinaNet was designed to
address those shortcomings

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:45.235
and to be both
fast and accurate.

00:13:45.235 --> 00:13:47.110
MARTIN GORNER: And it's
a one-stage detector.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.210
NIKITA SPIRIN: It's
a one-stage detector,

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:53.620
and it uses the
ResNet as a backbone.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.250
And it also relies on
the pyramid structure

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:59.380
of the neural
network and the fact

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:02.740
that it has inside pooling
layers that gradually

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:05.050
shrink the size of the image.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:10.060
And it is helpful because, when
we want to do object detection,

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.630
we want to be able to recognize
objects at different sizes,

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:15.160
at different resolutions.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:19.570
And by having the natural
structure of the ResNet

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:23.540
pyramid, we generate multiple
layers of representation.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.440
And this is called
Feature Pyramid Network,

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.540
the idea that was
also introduced

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:31.270
in the paper on
feature pyramids.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:36.940
And we use that multi-resolution
representation combination

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.000
of those feature
maps as our image.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.360
And then, we extend
with two subnetworks.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:47.090
One subnetwork is responsible
for classification.

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:50.260
And this is basically
to assign class label.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:53.470
And the second part is
responsible for regression,

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:57.160
where we use special type
of parametrization, which

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:01.600
was introduced in "Faster
R-CNN" paper, called anchors.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.490
So I will not go
into the details,

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:05.890
but the idea here is that
we have bounding boxes,

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.830
and we adjust the coordinates
of the bounding boxes using

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:09.760
the neural net.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:13.040
And that's the
architecture that we use.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.790
And obviously, then, we
launched the training process.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:17.790
MARTIN GORNER: And so
this architecture actually

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:20.200
existed before RetinaNet,
but you told me

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.360
it has one problem--

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.730
cold-class imbalance.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:25.270
What's that?

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:27.610
NIKITA SPIRIN: Let's talk
about it a little bit.

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:31.090
Object detection-- as
you can see on the slide,

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:33.040
there are only two pandas.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:35.650
And those are true positions
of the bounding boxes

00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:37.150
around them, in red.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:39.460
And there are a
lot of background

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:42.160
bounding boxes in blue.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:45.140
And those are negative
examples for machine learning.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:48.280
So when you train a neural
net, the loss function

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:51.940
that you have, if you sum
over all potential positions

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.450
of bounding boxes,
there are easy cases.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:58.060
Or the cases coming
from the background--

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:01.900
they will saturate the loss
function so that it will be not

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.890
sensitive to the true objectives
that we tried to learn from

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.120
the data set--

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:09.370
how to predict the
position and class

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.053
label for real bounding boxes.

00:16:11.053 --> 00:16:12.970
MARTIN GORNER: And when
you say they are easy,

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.240
you mean that the network
makes a small error on them.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.350
But small error multiplied by
hundreds of background boxes

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:25.020
becomes big error, and the
pandas get lost in that.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.970
And RetinaNet introduced
a very clever idea

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:31.540
to overcome that,
called a focal loss.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.740
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah, and focal
loss, just for you to remember,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:37.810
comes from the word
"focus" because it actually

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.330
focuses on hard
examples, so examples

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:44.420
which are difficult to
classify for the neural net.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:50.800
And in terms of math, it extends
the traditional cross-entropy

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:55.360
loss by aiding the
multiplication factor, 1

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:59.920
minus p to the power of gamma.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:03.250
And then, the way it
works, essentially,

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:06.369
if the probability
is high, which

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:10.450
means that in this example,
then the multiplicative factor

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:12.490
will be very, very
small, especially

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:13.839
if we exponentiate it.

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:15.849
And the contribution
of that easy example,

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:19.750
or the background bounding
box, will be very, very minimal

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:21.140
to the loss function.

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.140
And if it's the
opposite, then it

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:25.310
will be kept in
the loss function.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:26.800
And because of
that, the neural net

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.100
will be really able to learn
the important signal of how

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:33.965
to focus on the hard examples.

00:17:33.965 --> 00:17:35.590
MARTIN GORNER: So
that's a clever idea,

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:38.230
but this actually works.

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:42.310
In this picture, you
have various variants

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:46.690
of RetinaNet in color.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:51.790
And the letters are various
competing architectures.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:55.360
And this is a
accuracy-versus-speed diagram.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:59.500
You see that I put a red line in
there for better understanding.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.428
RetinaNet is above everything.

00:18:01.428 --> 00:18:03.970
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah, and as we
say in the research community,

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:09.160
it's in the upper
envelope of the graph.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:10.880
MARTIN GORNER: We
have good hardware.

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:12.910
We have a great model.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:16.090
What is missing, now, is
a good working environment

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:21.080
for data scientists to put
this model through its phases.

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:23.410
And for that, I would
like to show you

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:29.350
a new feature in AI Platform,
which is called Notebooks.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:33.150
So, AI Platform used
to be called ML Engine.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.100
As we are adding feature,
we renamed it AI Platform.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.680
If you're used to Jobs
and deployed Models,

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.920
the new feature
here is Notebooks.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:45.010
In Notebooks, you can
create a new instance

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:49.900
that has everything installed
in either TensorFlow or PyTorch.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:51.990
And you can select
that you want it--

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.520
you want just a standard
instance with CPUs,

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:58.630
or you can get a GPU
with it, or even a TPU.

00:18:58.630 --> 00:19:00.910
I'll show you how in a second.

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:05.230
What you get is an instance
and a link, a fast link,

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:09.160
to open Jupyter directly
on your instance.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.630
Actually, I've done that.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.330
That's why it's complaining.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:16.600
So here I am in my familiar
Jupyter environment.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:20.230
And actually, in
this environment,

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:23.500
this machine is
actually TPU powered.

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:25.580
Since you have not
seen it in the UI,

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:29.530
how to create a Jupyter
machine with a TPU,

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.270
let me give you the cheat code.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.380
You can do so on
the command line.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.160
So I have one command
line to launch my VM,

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:38.620
and the second one
to create a TPU,

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:42.160
and a little bit of glue
here to connect the two.

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.710
It will soon be in the UI.

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:48.470
In the meantime, this is
how you can get there.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:51.250
So let's do something
in this environment.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:54.440
Let's have a look at some data.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:59.320
First of all, I want to show you
how to load data because TPUs

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.100
are very fast, which means
that it happens very quickly

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:06.340
that you get data stored.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.130
And you have a limit on how
fast your training, simply

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:11.490
because the data is not
getting there fast enough.

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:18.260
So in here, in this
notebook, I have

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:25.180
a first cell that loads the
annotations of this data set.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:28.760
300,000 images-- those are
the annotations of the boxes.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.850
You see it's a
wildlife-specific data set.

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:33.980
Here are all the categories.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.740
It can recognize mouses,
hamsters, snow leopards,

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.150
and so on.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:40.820
And what I like
about Notebooks is

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:42.650
that I can visualize
my statistics

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:44.090
and eyeball my data sets.

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:45.830
So for example,
I see that I have

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:48.590
a couple of fairly large
images in this data set.

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:51.420
Maybe I will have to
pay attention to that.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:55.740
But now, I want
to see my images.

00:20:55.740 --> 00:21:00.650
So for that, I'm going to
use the tf.data.Dataset API.

00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:03.920
That's an API that is
designed to prepare

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:06.860
data sets for ingestion
by a neural network.

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:11.000
I'm starting with,
here, list_files.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:15.860
And from a bucket
on GCS, I'm getting

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.440
a data set of file names.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:22.290
Then, in this API, you can map
any function on your data set.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:24.950
So I'm mapping a decoding
function, which is here.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:29.180
It just basically loads the
image and calls decode_jpeg.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:30.980
And now, from my data
set of file names,

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:34.260
I have a data set
of images in memory.

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:38.910
I will also need to map
and resize my images.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:43.180
And then I have all the tools
I need to work on my data set,

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.010
so I can repeat it
across multiple epochs.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:46.940
I can shuffle it.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:49.550
I can batch my data
set for training.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:53.060
And a nice one is prefetch.

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:55.490
With prefetch, you
are telling the system

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:58.280
to work on the next
batch while you are

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:00.210
training on the current batch.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:02.660
So you can do all the
preprocessing in parallel,

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:06.110
with training with prefetch.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:10.010
And in TensorFlow,
the way you can

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.230
iterate on a data set
to see what is inside

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.850
is simply by creating a loop.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.630
This works in either
TensorFlow 2.0,

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:21.050
or if you activate eager
mode in TensorFlow.

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:21.770
And here I am.

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:24.990
Here are my image
tensors coming out.

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:26.115
So 16 by 16--

00:22:26.115 --> 00:22:26.960
OK.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.320
Batches of 16 384 by 384 images.

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:33.400
But you see this
is painfully slow,

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:35.600
and they're coming
like one every second--

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:36.350
super slow.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:38.420
Why is that?

00:22:38.420 --> 00:22:42.520
We have 300,000 images,
and they are on GCS.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:45.140
GCS can get you
great throughput,

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:48.560
but GCS, as any storage
that is attached

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:53.330
through IP, the internet, will
have a penalty for every file

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:54.800
you access.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:23:01.300
So getting 300,000 files one by
one is not a good idea at all--

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:02.600
won't work.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:05.390
And, yeah, I'm visualizing them
so you see what kind of images

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.640
I have.

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:12.380
The solution is to batch
your files into bigger files.

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:18.380
And for that, we use a file
format code called a TFRecords.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.210
We use it not because it
has super special features,

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:25.730
but because it can put multiple
pieces of data in one file,

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:29.330
and it has good support
in the data set library.

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:32.480
So let me show
you how to use it.

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:34.370
Nik and his team--
they've done the work

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:37.820
of putting those
files into TFRecords.

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:41.090
So now, I'm reading
here from a bucket

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.370
which has TFRecord files.

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:46.310
And it starts, again,
with list_files.

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:48.200
I have a list of file names.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:50.210
Then, I apply--

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.980
I create from that,
a TFRecorddataset.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:56.720
Now, I have a data
set of records.

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:58.310
This line is commented
out because I

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:03.710
do advise you to use this
interleave command to apply

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:06.770
Recorddataset because,
with this, you are

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.900
reading from 16 files at once.

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:13.100
And that is how you get a
good throughput from GCS.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:17.360
You put your data into
a reasonable number

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:21.560
of reasonably large files,
like 100 megabytes large,

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.270
and then you read
from multiple files

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:26.360
at once using the data set API.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:28.950
After that, I'm mapping
a decoding function,

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:30.020
which is here.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:34.190
And here, basically, I'm
reading from every records,

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:36.810
all the stuff that
Nik has put in there.

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:39.650
So that has the
image, some metadata,

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:41.780
and all my bounding
boxes, and the classes

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:44.870
of those bounding boxes.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:48.270
And so that's basically it.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:54.860
So now, again, I can
visualize my images.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:59.180
And let me relaunch,
here, a loop that

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.110
will pull images, 16 by 16.

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.440
So here is my training data set.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:06.320
You see it has pictures
and bounding boxes--

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.700
dolphins, ladybugs, and so on.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:12.200
And this time, as you can
see, my batches of 16 images

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:14.420
are coming out aesthetically.

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:16.070
This is the speed you want.

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:19.890
NIKITA SPIRIN: And just
pause here for a second.

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.820
This is one design pattern,
or program pattern,

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.510
that we want you to remember,
to have a reasonable number

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:28.070
of reasonably large files.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:34.910
A reasonable number of files is
about 110 to 100, and in size,

00:25:34.910 --> 00:25:36.980
also, 10 to 100 megabytes.

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:40.190
In this case, you will be
able to really maximize

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:44.930
the throughput and benefit
from tf.data.Dataset parallel

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:45.860
loading.

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.830
One additional tip
that we want to share

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:52.430
is about the bounding boxes and
the nuances of the RetinaNet

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:55.160
implementation, especially
for the data scientists

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:56.180
in the room.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:26:00.380
For managers, it's just
hard, and data scientists

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:02.090
should be valued.

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:04.730
There was a caveat in the
implementation of RetinaNet

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.310
from the official
report, where there

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:11.120
was a limit on the number
of bounding boxes, to 100.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:13.370
Somewhere hidden in
the code we started,

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:15.680
we thought that it will
just work off the bad.

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:20.210
And it was silently stopping,
and training was failing.

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:24.740
And the practical
suggestion for you

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:28.670
is to filter out the bounding
boxes from the images

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:31.140
if it's not very critical
for your application.

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:32.210
So you have an image.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:34.820
Either you skip
it, or you remove

00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:36.890
the number of bounding
boxes per image

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:39.710
so that this
pipeline can be used.

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:44.120
Obviously, if you
really want to build

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:47.760
a detector that is capturing
a lot of different objects--

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:49.760
for example, it could
be people tracking

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.920
for security
applications-- then you

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:53.480
have to work around on that.

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:56.960
But a short fix
is know, at least,

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:59.930
that there are 100 bounding
boxes in the implementation,

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:02.030
as a hard setting.

00:27:02.030 --> 00:27:05.290
MARTIN GORNER: I
also want to show you

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:08.990
how to use a TPU with
your own Keras model.

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:12.520
So for that, let me use
the exact same data set.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:17.200
But I have just taken the
first class for every image,

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:19.840
and I will build a very
simple classifier just

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:23.950
to show you how to build
a Keras model using a TPU.

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:27.460
So same data set-- you recognize
the data set code here.

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:31.990
And the second
piece of the code is

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:36.730
here, a convolutional
classifier in Keras.

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:40.040
So it uses the
tf.keras.Sequential model.

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.700
And it's a sequence of
convolutional layers,

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:46.540
ending with a certain number
of classes, softmax activation.

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.550
So it's a classifier.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:53.200
Here is what Keras tells
me this model is made of.

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:56.050
And the three lines of code
that I would like to show you

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:58.390
are here.

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:03.010
First, you have one
function to find your TPU.

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:07.570
Then, there is a second function
to define a TPU distribution

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.820
strategy using that TPU--

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:11.230
why distribution?

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:13.612
One TPU is eight cores.

00:28:13.612 --> 00:28:15.030
Eight.

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:18.190
So it's distributed training--

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:22.210
and a third function that
transforms your Keras model

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:25.180
into a model that can
be trained on TPU.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:28.800
So those three lines allow
you to use TPUs in Keras.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:33.310
And if I run them, you
will see here, in the logs

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:36.370
that my eight TPU cores that
are showing up, this notebook

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.160
is actually powered by TPU v3.

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:40.690
And then I can
launch the training.

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:43.270
Well, there will be
nothing interesting here.

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:46.210
Normal Keras
training will start.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:48.340
It takes just a couple
of seconds to start.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:52.360
I think that we can
move on because we

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:54.840
have other interesting
things to tell you.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:55.340
Yeah.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:56.548
Yeah, you see, it's starting.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:05.210
So just to recap, this is how
you use TPUs in a Keras model.

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:08.000
Three simple lines--
find your TPU,

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:11.000
define a distribution
strategy that uses that TPU,

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:14.420
and then keras_to_tpu_model to
get a model that you can train.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:18.100
And when you train it,
it will run on TPU.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:20.610
A quick word of
what is going on--

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:23.660
so this is your VM, your
TensorFlow Python code.

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:25.130
That's what it's running.

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:27.710
You remember that
Python and Keras--

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:31.330
they represent your neural
network as a computation graph,

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:32.630
in memory.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:36.350
And it's that computation
graph that is sent to the TPU.

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:39.050
Your TPU never sees
your Python code.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:41.180
That's important
to bear in mind.

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:43.730
The TPUs, they don't
execute Python code.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:46.040
They get the computation graph.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:49.820
They use the Accelerated
Linear Algebra compiler, XLA,

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:54.540
to transform that computation
graph into TPU byte code.

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:58.040
And if you're using
the data set API,

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:00.590
then your data input
pipeline is also

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:02.720
a piece of TensorFlow
graph, which

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.760
will be shipped to the TPU.

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:08.240
And during training, the TPU
will be pulling data directly

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:09.440
from GCS.

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:12.170
So that's quite efficient.

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:15.050
And then, finally,
this is the code

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:18.080
for reading from TFRecord files.

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:21.020
Please, if you don't care
about ordering of your data,

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:23.600
which you never do-- you
usually shuffle your data--

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:26.460
please don't forget to put
experimental_deterministic

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:28.170
= False.

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:30.140
Because this tells
the data set API

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:33.710
that it can do whatever it wants
with the data to get it fast.

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:36.680
And then, you list your files.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:40.400
From a data set of file names,
you get a data set of records.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:44.660
You map a decoding
function to get a data

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:46.130
set of whatever you care about.

00:30:46.130 --> 00:30:49.340
And then you can shuffle, batch,
repeat, prefetch your data.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:53.330
And also please use
the interleave function

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:58.040
to apply your TFRecordDataset
transformation,

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:00.470
because that's how
you can load your data

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:02.015
from 16 files at once.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:09.600
So Nik, how do you like
having a powerful TPU

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:12.630
v3 at your fingertips,
in a notebook?

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:15.120
NIKITA SPIRIN: I think having
a powerful accelerator is

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:16.770
definitely amazing.

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:21.060
At the same time, we don't want
to pay for powerful machines

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:22.410
just to type code.

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:26.490
And we also don't want to run
around and chase machines that

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:29.400
are idle and don't do any
useful work because we

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:30.540
have to pay for it.

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:33.828
MARTIN GORNER: And how did you
solve that problem at Gigster?

00:31:33.828 --> 00:31:35.370
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah,
when we started,

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.380
we were ahead of
the game, and we

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:40.050
had to invent some
solutions from scratch.

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:45.270
And we built, so-called, GDE,
which is Gigster Development

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:47.880
Environment, and a set
of processes around it,

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:50.250
to make sure that our data
scientists on the team

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:53.710
are productive, and also that
we minimize the infrastructure

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:54.660
cost.

00:31:54.660 --> 00:31:55.620
The way it works--

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:59.310
we have a few machines
allocated to the team

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:01.770
for quick interactive
experiments.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:05.910
And we also have
the Dockerized jobs

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:09.780
that we start for training real
production workflow models.

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:12.210
So we have a lot of
Terraform scripts,

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:14.520
Ansible scripts that
provision the machines,

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:18.490
install necessary environment
variables, drivers, and so on,

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:23.100
and then take Docker
container, run the job out

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:25.590
of the Docker container, and
save the model to the cloud

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:26.490
storage.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:29.040
And that took us a lot
of effort to build.

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:32.320
And it would be nice if
it comes out of the box,

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:33.930
or almost out of the box.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.400
MARTIN GORNER: Yes,
Ansible Terraform scripts--

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:38.737
that doesn't sound like
data science, does it?

00:32:38.737 --> 00:32:39.570
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah.

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:40.650
It's not data science.

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:41.880
It's DevOps.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.680
We have a great set
DevOps engineers.

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:45.805
But yes--

00:32:45.805 --> 00:32:47.430
MARTIN GORNER: I
would like to show you

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:54.045
a new API that just came out,
where a data scientist can--

00:32:54.045 --> 00:32:55.950
that a data scientist
can to use to launch

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:59.760
his notebook as a job,
without doing a lot of work.

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.530
It's called fairing.

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:04.500
And that's what I used, at the
beginning, to launch my job.

00:33:04.500 --> 00:33:08.640
And now, let me show
you exactly what it is.

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:16.990
So first of all, this is
fairing is the cap of a rocket.

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:17.490
OK?

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:19.038
It's where you put the payloads.

00:33:19.038 --> 00:33:19.830
So that's the idea.

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:23.435
You have a payload, and you want
to launch this payload as a job

00:33:23.435 --> 00:33:25.810
so that you don't have to
worry about the infrastructure.

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:27.420
It will spin up
the infrastructure.

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:29.730
It will tier it down at the end.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:31.180
What is my payload?

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:32.230
So here is my payload.

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:33.090
It's in Notebook.

00:33:33.090 --> 00:33:38.090
Looks very much like the
previous notebook I showed you.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:41.520
And sure, let's call
this one ONSTAGE2.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:43.800
What it does is that--

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:48.490
well, it does some exploratory
analysis of the data set.

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:50.610
Maybe it displays
a couple of images.

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:52.560
Then I have a training cell.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:55.290
And then when the model,
my model, has trained,

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.780
I have an inference
cell that does something

00:33:57.780 --> 00:33:59.250
with the trained model.

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:02.130
That's fairly typical
of the way I use

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:04.370
Notebooks to work on models.

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:07.240
But now, my model is
in a fairly good shape.

00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:09.210
I want to launch its
training as a job.

00:34:09.210 --> 00:34:12.300
And usually, you don't
want to do this once.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:14.580
You are going to launch it
once with this parameter,

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:16.260
and a second time
with this parameter,

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:18.550
and then a fourth,
and then a fifth time.

00:34:18.550 --> 00:34:20.800
And very quickly, you have
multiple jobs running,

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:24.090
which is something you would
not be able to do on one Jupyter

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:25.540
machine.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:29.040
So this is the fairing API.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.150
It has three components.

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:35.520
The first one is
to know what are

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:37.429
the requirements of your job.

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:41.250
For that, the easiest is to
have a Docker file describing

00:34:41.250 --> 00:34:42.870
the environment.

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:44.400
I was kind of scared of that.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:45.659
It's not my world.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:49.020
But my colleagues showed me that
the Docker file was basically

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:53.370
these four lines,
listing the pip

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:55.060
installs that I needed to do.

00:34:55.060 --> 00:34:55.560
OK.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:57.120
I can write this.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:02.250
And then, the fairing API,
itself, has those three calls.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:05.130
So one call--
set_builder-- to specify

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:09.120
what is your base environment,
the Docker file from which it

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:11.160
will be building your job.

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:14.400
A second, one, set_deployer--
here I'm saying GCP.

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:17.950
GCP means AI Platform--
a job on AI Platform.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:22.920
And I can specify here what
kind of hardware I want.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:26.450
You see here, I'm
asking for a TPU v3.

00:35:26.450 --> 00:35:28.680
TPU v3.

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.680
And finally, a third
line specifying what

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:33.600
is the payload--

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:35.310
one option is to
have a full notebook.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.170
Of course, if it is
just Python scripts,

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:39.370
that works just as well.

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:41.550
But if I'm selecting
a full notebook,

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:46.020
I can specify my notebook
file and if I have any number

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:48.030
of sidecar files that I need.

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:51.690
For example, here, my
RetinaNet Python files--

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:54.540
I can specify a list
of those and then

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:58.020
where I want to put my output.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:03.810
When I run this, what happens
is that all these things

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:07.980
get packaged, automatically,
into another Docker container

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:11.040
and a job is launched
on AI platform jobs,

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.560
using that Docker container.

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:14.190
What is it?

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:15.790
Here, it started.

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:17.970
Let me check that in my jobs.

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:20.280
Now, I haven't a
second job running,

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.930
launched, four seconds ago.

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:24.030
So I can stop this one.

00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:24.840
I don't need it.

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:27.820
I already launched
it at the beginning.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:32.058
And maybe we can check if our
model has finished training.

00:36:32.058 --> 00:36:32.600
So let's see.

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:41.270
I should have here, right here--

00:36:41.270 --> 00:36:46.040
I have this folder ONSTAGE,
and it has finished training.

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:49.710
This numbered folder
contains a saved model.

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:54.965
So let's see what
this model looks like.

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:01.070
I need to download this.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:05.500
I'm going to upload it here
so that we can see it--

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:07.380
Downloads, ONSTAGE.

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:10.480
OK.

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:12.040
It's coming up.

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:14.200
It will take a minute to upload.

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:17.300
You see if I do this, it
tells me it hasn't finished.

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:21.310
So what I want to show you here
is that the result of my job--

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:23.560
usually when I launch
a job from a notebook,

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:25.610
I have to extract some
code from my notebook,

00:37:25.610 --> 00:37:31.790
and then I lose my nice
environment, which I'm used.

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:35.650
Here, the result of the job
is a fully-rendered notebook.

00:37:35.650 --> 00:37:37.660
So everything I have
before training,

00:37:37.660 --> 00:37:41.380
playing with the data, all the
visualizations, will be there.

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:43.700
My training will be
there with all the logs,

00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:45.280
if I want to check something.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:49.120
Anything I do after training,
like playing with a trained

00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:51.550
model, eyeballing
that it's actually

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:53.140
recognizing my animals--

00:37:53.140 --> 00:37:55.760
all that will be there as well.

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:56.950
So let's see.

00:37:56.950 --> 00:37:57.790
Now it's opening.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.930
And here we go.

00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:08.240
So if I scroll down, my
exploratory data analysis

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:08.740
is here.

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:10.570
I see my classes.

00:38:10.570 --> 00:38:13.780
I see my training data
with the bounding boxes.

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:17.500
I can make sure I didn't
screw up my image resizing

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:20.260
and throw my bounding boxes off.

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:21.940
I have my training cell.

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:26.020
I have, of course, all
the training logs here.

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:29.170
At the end, I can check
that this whole training--

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:34.480
so full ResNet-50-backed
RetinaNet,

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:38.110
trained on 300,000
images on a TPU v3--

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:41.280
took 28 minutes and
50 seconds to train.

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:43.280
And, of course, you want
to know how good it is.

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.990
So let's see on a
couple of images.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:49.390
Here, I'm showing your
evaluation images.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:51.640
So on eval images,
in the white outline,

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:53.500
you have the ground truth.

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:55.210
And you see this fox--

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:56.670
pretty good.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:38:58.390
It did the elephant.

00:38:58.390 --> 00:39:01.150
I'm not cherry
picking my examples.

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:03.310
So you will see false
detections here.

00:39:03.310 --> 00:39:04.720
There is no person.

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:07.170
And the chimp is not a bear.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:08.790
The zebra-- OK.

00:39:08.790 --> 00:39:12.100
The polar bear is nice.

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:15.100
Here, it got the
leopard, the tiger--

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:18.850
a little bit of an
error on the tiger.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:20.810
The baby pandas are hard.

00:39:20.810 --> 00:39:22.960
It doesn't have a lot of
babies in the data set.

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:23.830
So it got one.

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:26.060
I'm actually quite
happy about that.

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:30.340
In this fairly busy scene,
it sees horses and cattles.

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:32.360
So that's quite nice as well.

00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:33.250
What else do I have?

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:36.530
I have a jaguar here.

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:38.210
Nobody's complaining?

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.890
Hey, this jaguar is
actually a leopard.

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:42.190
OK.

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:43.780
I didn't know the
difference either

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:46.270
before I started working
with this data set.

00:39:46.270 --> 00:39:49.060
We can forgive this
ONSTAGE build model

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:50.690
for not spotting that.

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:53.660
It gives me snow leopard
there, a lion, and so on.

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:56.500
So actually, not bad
at all for something

00:39:56.500 --> 00:39:59.350
that we trained on stage,
here, in less than 30 minutes.

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:08.170
This was fairing-- a way
of taking any payload,

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:11.770
but notebook-friendly,
and launch it as a job.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:16.870
Just a little productivity
tool for you data scientists--

00:40:16.870 --> 00:40:18.520
you say where you
want to deploy,

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:21.980
you say what hardware
architecture you want--

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:23.732
this can be a whole cluster--

00:40:23.732 --> 00:40:25.690
and you say what you want
to deploy and you go.

00:40:25.690 --> 00:40:26.690
SPEAKER: Good afternoon.

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:30.422
The expo floor will be
closing in 15 minutes.

00:40:30.422 --> 00:40:33.442
Please make your way into the
escalators in the main lobby.

00:40:33.442 --> 00:40:35.900
Thank you.

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:37.891
MARTIN GORNER: Thank you too.

00:40:37.891 --> 00:40:40.370
So Nik, we have a good hardware.

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:41.510
