is Stochastic Gradient Descent.

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:12.140
So by having access
to TPUs and fairing,

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:15.170
we were able to explore a lot
of different hyperparameters,

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:18.533
train many instances
of our neural nets.

00:41:18.533 --> 00:41:21.200
MARTIN GORNER: And maybe you can
tell us where you started from.

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:22.180
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:26.910
I will cover it as we go.

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:31.490
So as you can see, we show some
interesting variations here,

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:34.430
among all the
experiments that we did.

00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:37.420
First thing to highlight
here is that we

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:41.480
started from the
RetinaNet implementation,

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:44.990
not from the TensorFlow
official repo optimized for TPU.

00:41:44.990 --> 00:41:48.500
It was just some other
implementation of RetinaNet.

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:53.750
And it took us about three days
to train this model on GPU.

00:41:53.750 --> 00:41:57.320
I would not say that the
order, or two orders,

00:41:57.320 --> 00:42:00.080
of magnitude speed
up is the thing

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:03.020
that we want to communicate
to you here, thanks to TPUs.

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:05.270
MARTIN GORNER: That's because
that model-- you told me

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:06.200
it had some issues.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:08.360
You did a little bit
of investigation.

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:12.130
You had to train it at a
batch size of two, right?

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:16.460
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah, there was
a problem with the batch size.

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:19.470
There was a problem because
of the memory limits.

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:22.130
And we only stick with
batch sizes of two.

00:42:22.130 --> 00:42:24.740
And also, I want
to say that when

00:42:24.740 --> 00:42:26.810
we were delivering
to the customer,

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:28.910
we had to deliver over
20 different models.

00:42:28.910 --> 00:42:31.310
And we had a very
aggressive timeline.

00:42:31.310 --> 00:42:35.360
So we shifted to
production and decided

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:37.910
that we will get back to it
during their second iteration

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:38.750
of the product.

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:40.790
And after that, we
partnered with Google

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:44.070
to start using TPUs
for the process.

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:45.470
MARTIN GORNER: So on TPU--

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:49.050
NIKITA SPIRIN: So the thing
that we want to highlight here--

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:51.590
it took us about
$200 and three days

00:42:51.590 --> 00:42:54.830
to train a model of
comparable quality

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:59.210
that we were able to
get with TPUs for $7.

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:01.220
So that's a great value.

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:03.080
And obviously,
RetinaNet optimized

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:07.470
for TPU is a great
contribution to that number.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:09.620
The second thing that
we wanted to highlight

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:14.150
is the best model that we got--

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:18.470
it is the highest ever accuracy
in mAP, one of the metrics

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:21.350
that is used for evaluation
of the quality of the object

00:43:21.350 --> 00:43:22.250
detector.

00:43:22.250 --> 00:43:28.050
We used our favorite
TPU v3, image size, 512.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.700
And we trained it for 10
epochs, while, in the past,

00:43:31.700 --> 00:43:35.418
we, I think, did three
or five epochs only,

00:43:35.418 --> 00:43:36.960
just because of the
time limitations.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:40.460
We didn't have the luxury
to train our neural nets

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:42.470
and wait until all
the experiments finish

00:43:42.470 --> 00:43:45.590
until we find a great
combination of hyperparameters.

00:43:45.590 --> 00:43:47.810
And here, you see
that with TPUs, we

00:43:47.810 --> 00:43:51.620
were able to train it into
ours to get this quality.

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:53.000
So that's a win.

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:57.470
And we are super excited to show
you this model here, on stage,

00:43:57.470 --> 00:43:58.230
as well.

00:43:58.230 --> 00:43:59.690
This is the model
that's currently

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:03.950
integrated into the product,
and it's serving real customers.

00:44:03.950 --> 00:44:05.720
MARTIN GORNER: So let's see.

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:11.490
This one-- here in my notebook,
I have a little utility-- oops,

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:12.800
please go up--

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:17.300
where I can-- so let me restart
this from the beginning.

00:44:17.300 --> 00:44:21.020
And instead of training,
I will just say, please

00:44:21.020 --> 00:44:23.330
reload from a trained model.

00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:26.960
It's a 512 pixel model.

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:31.040
And I will simply
skip the training part

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:33.300
and go directly to inference.

00:44:33.300 --> 00:44:33.800
Here we go.

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:38.040
So now, the model is--

00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:39.350
it will get there in--

00:44:39.350 --> 00:44:40.410
it's reloading.

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:43.620
It's reloading
from a saved model.

00:44:43.620 --> 00:44:47.910
And then, again, I will
be getting random images.

00:44:47.910 --> 00:44:50.830
Not cherry picking anything--
random images from my eval data

00:44:50.830 --> 00:44:51.330
set.

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:55.250
Now it's computing the
boxes on those images.

00:44:55.250 --> 00:44:57.600
And, you see, the
chimp is perfect.

00:44:57.600 --> 00:44:59.560
The polar bear is perfect.

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:01.530
You still have, in
white, the ground truth,

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:04.140
on this eval data set.

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.520
What else do we have?

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:06.790
That's probably not a ray.

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:08.160
It looks like a shark.

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:10.460
We have a gorilla here.

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:11.940
The wolf, I'm not sure.

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:13.870
But the Komodo
