00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:37.460
MARTIN GORNER: [CHUCKLES]
So this was the model.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:40.500
And I think what we
wanted to highlight

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:44.700
is that speed velocity
of your data science team

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:46.560
is not just for the comfort.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:49.230
It's for the cost
of what you pay.

00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:52.980
But also, you can experiment
more and get better results.

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:55.538
And you actually did even
more experimentation.

00:45:55.538 --> 00:45:57.330
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah,
more experimentation--

00:45:57.330 --> 00:46:01.080
it's how to use
TPUs for inference.

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:04.230
And by default, we
already know that TPUs

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:08.010
are very powerful machines,
and they expect big batches.

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:10.710
While, when we serve
models in production,

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:13.590
the typical workflow
or load pattern

00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:16.890
is to have one image at a
time hits API endpoint video

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:18.580
processing, and so on.

00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:21.010
And if we use TPUs
in that scenario,

00:46:21.010 --> 00:46:24.570
probably, that will not fully
utilize the power of TPU.

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:27.840
We thought about
some other workflow

00:46:27.840 --> 00:46:30.630
that will benefit from TPUs.

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:34.690
And actually, we found this
workflow in the project.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:38.340
It's not the theoretical
workflow, or just workflow,

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:40.140
for this presentation.

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:44.460
In our case, we had a data set
of over 100 million images,

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:46.650
and we had to build a
visual search solution

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:48.420
on top of that data set.

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:49.570
So what we did--

00:46:49.570 --> 00:46:52.920
we had a pre-trained neural net.

00:46:52.920 --> 00:46:56.190
We passed all images
through this neural net

00:46:56.190 --> 00:46:57.480
to generate embeddings.

00:46:57.480 --> 00:47:03.330
Then, we indexed that data
set of vectors using the phase

00:47:03.330 --> 00:47:04.180
package.

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:06.780
And then we got the
nearest-neighbor approximate

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:07.860
search built in.

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:09.990
The entire stack
supports visual search.

00:47:09.990 --> 00:47:14.110
And for that purpose,
we, again, used TPU v3.

00:47:14.110 --> 00:47:16.200
And as you can see
on the slide, it

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:20.610
is the fastest and the cheapest
alternative to make sure

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:25.050
that we can complete this very
complex, massive indexing job,

00:47:25.050 --> 00:47:28.290
a job that combines the
generation of embeddings

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:31.150
and supporting
the visual search.

00:47:31.150 --> 00:47:32.400
MARTIN GORNER: Thank you, Nik.

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:37.230
So we talked here about
building neural networks,

00:47:37.230 --> 00:47:41.250
optimizing them, but there
is actually a lot, lot more,

00:47:41.250 --> 00:47:43.500
in a full-blown
project, than that.

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:45.500
This is kind of the
workflow you're using.

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:45.860
It's very complicated.

00:47:45.860 --> 00:47:46.680
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah,
this is the workflow

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:48.780
that we used to develop
for the enterprise.

00:47:48.780 --> 00:47:51.290
And it's very different
from having a Jupyter

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:54.780
Notebook on a laptop, or even
connected to the cloud machine.

00:47:54.780 --> 00:47:57.540
We have the environment
where our data scientists do

00:47:57.540 --> 00:47:59.190
training and experimentation.

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:01.470
Then, we upgraded
to the environment

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:03.750
where we demo our
models to the customer

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:06.030
every two weeks, just
following the agile iterations.

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:09.450
And we have some
representation of the customer.

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:11.940
I will not start pointing.

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:16.800
Then, we upgrade our
models into the environment

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:19.620
where we do the first
integration of machine learning

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:22.060
with the web mobile
part of the product.

00:48:22.060 --> 00:48:26.850
This is where we do the
testing at the program level.

00:48:26.850 --> 00:48:28.760
And then, we start
already moving

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:31.230
to the client
environment, where we have

00:48:31.230 --> 00:48:32.628
upstaging and up-production.

00:48:32.628 --> 00:48:34.170
MARTIN GORNER: And
so what I see here

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:37.200
is that you need to
move all those workloads

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:40.217
between yourselves, and also
between you and the client.

00:48:40.217 --> 00:48:41.050
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah.

00:48:41.050 --> 00:48:43.830
It's very critical for
Gigster because we first build

00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:46.120
it using our talent network.

00:48:46.120 --> 00:48:48.960
And then, we have to hand
over code to the client,

00:48:48.960 --> 00:48:52.440
and also make sure that they
can maintain that code base,

00:48:52.440 --> 00:48:54.465
contribute to that code
base, moving forward.

00:48:54.465 --> 00:48:56.340
MARTIN GORNER: I think
you have a perfect use

00:48:56.340 --> 00:48:57.530
case for work for Kubeflow.

00:48:57.530 --> 00:48:59.190
NIKITA SPIRIN: Yeah,
we are currently

00:48:59.190 --> 00:49:00.330
looking into Kubeflow.

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.760
We are not using it
right now, but this

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:07.140
is one thing that could
help us and you standardize

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:09.420
the development
of the pipelines,

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:12.120
and make it sharable
within your organization,

00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:14.190
and also with the
client organization.

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:16.930
MARTIN GORNER: Shareable
and reproducible.

00:49:16.930 --> 00:49:18.318
So just a shout out to Kubeflow.

00:49:18.318 --> 00:49:19.860
If you're facing
